{
  "id": "ecoli_tryptophan_biosynthesis",
  "name": "Tryptophan Biosynthesis via Anthranilate",
  "organism": "E. coli",
  "category": "Metabolic Pathway",
  "description": "Tryptophan biosynthesis in E. coli is a 5-step pathway from chorismate (shikimate pathway product) requiring 7 enzymes encoded by the trp operon. The pathway: chorismate + glutamine \u2192 anthranilate (TrpE/D) \u2192 phosphoribosyl-anthranilate (TrpD) \u2192 CDRP \u2192 indole-3-glycerol phosphate (TrpC) \u2192 tryptophan (TrpA/B). TrpB combines indole from TrpA with serine to form tryptophan. The trp operon is regulated by TrpR repressor (binds tryptophan) and attenuation (TRAP mechanism with leader sequence).",
  "scientificAccuracy": "Based on comprehensive biochemical and structural studies of tryptophan biosynthesis.",
